<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1776</h1><p class="ref-lede">1776 in world history: 40 events, 9 births, 8 deaths, 3 works, 5 states founded and 14 that came to an end. 1,296 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a><a href="/years/1773">1773</a><a href="/years/1774">1774</a><a href="/years/1775">1775</a><a href="/years/1776" aria-current="page">1776</a><a href="/years/1777">1777</a><a href="/years/1778">1778</a><a href="/years/1779">1779</a><a href="/years/1780">1780</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/boston" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boston.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Washington's army raises the Grand Union Flag at Prospect Hill</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">During the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, the Continental Army hoisted the Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Flag</a> on Prospect Hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Massachusetts. Combining thirteen stripes with the British Union, it served as the first flag of the united colonies.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Maria Theresa abolishes judicial torture in Austria</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Persuaded by reformers such as Joseph von Sonnenfels, Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> ended the use of torture in criminal proceedings in her Austrian and Bohemian lands. It was a landmark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> legal reform.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Thomas Paine publishes 'Common Sense'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Paine's plain-spoken pamphlet argued that the American colonies should declare independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It sold tens of thousands of copies within months and helped turn opinion towards independence.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-3">3 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Continental Marines land at Nassau in the Bahamas</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In their first amphibious operation, American marines and sailors captured two British forts on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> island, seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> and military stores.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/boston" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boston.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British troops evacuate Boston</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After Washington fortified Dorchester Heights with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> hauled from Fort Ticonderoga, General Howe's army sailed for Halifax. The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> ended in an American success.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April</a> · Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/halifax-resolves">Halifax Resolves</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Halifax Resolves were adopted by North Carolina's Fourth Provincial Congress at Halifax on 12 April 1776.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-1">1 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Adam Weishaupt founds the Bavarian Illuminati</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Law professor Adam Weishaupt founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas in Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It was banned in the 1780s but has lived on in conspiracy theories ever since.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Rhode Island renounces allegiance to George III</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Rhode Island's assembly became the first colonial legislature to formally renounce allegiance to the Crown, two months before the Declaration of Independence.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-7">7 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Richard Henry Lee moves for American independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Lee proposed that 'these United Colonies are, and of right ought to be, free and independent States'. Congress adopted the resolution on 2 July.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-11">11 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Congress appoints a committee to draft a declaration of independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, John Adams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, Roger Sherman and Robert Livingston formed the Committee of Five. Jefferson wrote the first draft.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-12">12 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Virginia adopts its Declaration of Rights</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Drafted mainly by George Mason, the Declaration set out rights such as freedom of the press and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>. It influenced the US Bill of Rights and the French Declaration of 1789.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sullivans-island" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sullivans-island.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June</a> · Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sullivans-island">Battle of Sullivan's Island</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Sullivan's Island was fought on 28 June 1776, when a British fleet under Sir Peter Parker attacked the unfinished palmetto-log fort on Sullivan's Island guarding Charleston harbour.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-29">29 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Spanish found Mission San Francisco de Asís</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Franciscan friars celebrated the first Mass at the mission later known as Mission <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> expanded northward in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. The settlement grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-1">1 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Continental Congress debates independence in a decisive test vote</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Meeting as a committee of the whole, Congress debated Richard Henry Lee's resolution for independence; nine colonies backed it in a test vote. The formal vote followed the next day with twelve in favour.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Continental Congress votes for independence from Britain</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress adopted the Lee Resolution declaring the colonies free and independent states, with twelve in favour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> abstaining. John Adams predicted that 2 July would be celebrated for ever; posterity chose the 4th.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-4">4 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Continental Congress adopts the Declaration of Independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Congress approved the final text of the Declaration, drafted chiefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>. Its assertion that all men are created equal, with unalienable rights, became the founding statement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Declaration of Independence is first read in public in Philadelphia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Colonel John Nixon read the Declaration to a crowd in the State House yard, now Independence Square. Tradition holds that the Liberty Bell rang out, though this story dates from the nineteenth century.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">New Yorkers topple George III's statue after the Declaration is read</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Washington had the Declaration read to his troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. That evening a crowd pulled down the gilded lead statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> on Bowling Green; much of the lead was later cast into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> balls.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-2">2 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Delegates sign the engrossed Declaration of Independence</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Most members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> signed the parchment copy of the Declaration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. By signing, they put their names to an act of treason in British eyes.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British forces defeat Washington at the Battle of Long Island</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">General William Howe outflanked and routed the Continental Army in Brooklyn, the largest battle of the war. Two nights later Washington's army escaped across the East River.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The submarine Turtle makes the first submarine attack</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Sergeant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> Lee steered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Bushnell's one-man submarine Turtle towards HMS Eagle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Harbor. He failed to attach the charge to the hull, but it was the first submarine attack in war.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Congress adopts the name 'United States of America'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> resolved that wherever 'United Colonies' had been used, the new nation should be styled the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>'. The name had appeared in the Declaration two months earlier.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-15">15 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British troops land at Kip's Bay and take New York</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After a heavy naval bombardment, British forces landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> at Kip's Bay, sending American troops fleeing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> stayed in British hands until 1783.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-16">16 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">American troops defeat the British at the Battle of Harlem Heights</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> campaign, just under 1,800 American troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> defeated just under 1,600 British troops under Brigadier-general Alexander Leslie on Harlem Heights, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Fire destroys up to 1,000 buildings in occupied New York</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Breaking out in the early days of the British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, the Great Fire burned through the night of 20 September into the morning of the 21st and destroyed up to 1,000 buildings.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-22">22 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The British hang Nathan Hale as a spy</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Captured behind British lines on Long Island, 21-year-old Captain Nathan Hale was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>. He is said to have regretted that he had 'but one life to lose for my country'.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">A British force defeats Benedict Arnold's fleet at Valcour Island</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a>, a British force under General Guy Carleton captured or destroyed most of the American fleet commanded by Benedict Arnold. The American defence of the lake nonetheless stalled British plans to reach the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> valley.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-16">16 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The British capture Fort Washington on Manhattan</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">British and Hessian troops stormed Fort Washington, the last American stronghold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, and took about 2,800 prisoners. Washington's army retreated across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British reinforcements end the Patriot siege of Fort Cumberland</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">British reinforcements ended a siege of Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> by Patriot militia under Jonathan Eddy. With four to five hundred volunteers and Natives and little support from Massachusetts, Eddy had tried to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Washington's army retreats across the Delaware</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The last of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>'s beaten Continental Army crossed the Delaware into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, taking every boat to stop British pursuit. Weeks later he crossed back to strike at Trenton.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-25">25 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Washington crosses the Delaware</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">On Christmas night, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> led about 2,400 soldiers across the ice-choked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a>. At dawn they surprised the Hessian garrison at Trenton.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-26">26 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Washington defeats the Hessians at Trenton</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Washington's army surprised the Hessian garrison at Trenton, capturing about 900 soldiers with few American losses. The victory revived the Revolution after months of defeat.</p></div></li></ol></section>
